This was based on the 3.15 PR: https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/pull/43129/files

Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

**Version Used**: 
.NET SDK 11.0.100-rc.1 / Roslyn 5.11.0 (`commit 3551975be08744f0418857c5bed8ab1545c5dd47`, also reproduced on current `main`)

**Steps to Reproduce**:

1. Run a parallel multi-project build (e.g. `dotnet test` or `dotnet build -m`) using the compiler server (`VBCSCompiler`).
2. Have multiple projects/target frameworks compiling concurrently through the same `VBCSCompiler` server instance.
3. Collect CLR contention events from `VBCSCompiler` using `dotnet-trace`:
   ```bash
   dotnet-trace collect --name VBCSCompiler --clrevents contention --clreventlevel verbose
   ```
4. Analyze the contention callstacks and thread states.

**Diagnostic Id**:
N/A (Compiler Server / CodeGen Concurrency Bottleneck)

**Expected Behavior**:
`VBCSCompiler` should efficiently scale when multiplexing concurrent project compilation requests across CPU cores, without threads spending massive amounts of wall-clock time blocked on compiler-internal locks.

**Actual Behavior**:
Severe lock contention occurs at `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CodeGen.TokenMap.AddItem` via `lock (_itemIdentityToToken)`. **This problem is dramatically exacerbated in `VBCSCompiler`**:

1. **Concurrent Compilation Requests in a Single Process**:
   * Unlike standalone `csc`, `VBCSCompiler` handles multiple MSBuild compilation requests simultaneously in the same process.
   * In our crash dump/thread analysis, we observed multiple instances of `MethodCompiler.CompileMethodBodies` executing concurrently (e.g., separate threads running `CompilerServerHost.RunCompilation` while waiting on their respective worker tasks).
2. **Thread Pool Saturation & Lock Convoy**:
   * Each active compilation schedules multiple method compilation tasks onto the shared `VBCSCompiler` thread pool (causing thread counts to surge to 90+ threads).
   * As dozens of worker threads across concurrent compilations emit IL instructions simultaneously, they all collide on `TokenMap.AddItem` locks.
3. **Trace Profile Data (20-second window on an active VBCSCompiler server)**:
   * **338 out of 520 total contention events (65.0%)** originated from `TokenMap.AddItem`.
   * Cumulative thread wait time on this single lock exceeded **100 seconds** within a 20-second real-time window (average wait ~296 ms per contention).

#### Contention Callstack:
```text
System.Threading.Lock.Enter / Monitor.Enter
  at Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CodeGen.TokenMap.AddItem(IReferenceOrISignature item, bool& referenceAdded)
  at Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CodeGen.TokenMap.GetOrAddTokenFor(IReference item, bool& referenceAdded)
  at Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Emit.CommonPEModuleBuilder.GetFakeSymbolTokenForIL(IReference item, SyntaxNode syntaxNode, DiagnosticBag diagnostics)
  at Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CodeGen.ILBuilder.EmitToken(IReference item, SyntaxNode syntaxNode, RawTokenEncoding encoding)
  at Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.CodeGen.CodeGenerator.EmitExpression / EmitStatement(...)
  at Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.MethodCompiler.GenerateMethodBody(...)
```

#### Cause in `src/Compilers/Core/Portable/CodeGen/TokenMap.cs`:
```csharp
private uint AddItem(IReferenceOrISignature item, out bool referenceAdded)
{
    uint token;
    lock (_itemIdentityToToken) // <-- Heavy global lock contention
    {
        if (!_itemIdentityToToken.TryGetValue(item, out token))
        {
            token = (uint)_count;
            referenceAdded = _itemIdentityToToken.TryAdd(item, token);
            Debug.Assert(referenceAdded);

            var count = (int)token + 1;
            var items = _items;
            if (items.Length > count)
            {
                items[(int)token] = item.AsObject();
            }
            else
            {
                Array.Resize(ref items, Math.Max(8, count * 2)); // Allocations & copy under lock
                items[(int)token] = item.AsObject();
                Volatile.Write(ref _items, items);
            }
            Volatile.Write(ref _count, count);
        }
        else { referenceAdded = false; }
    }
    return token;
}
```
* Even though `GetOrAddTokenFor` has a lock-free `TryGetValue`, initial method body compilation across multiple projects encounters thousands of distinct symbols/signatures that miss the fast path.
* Inside `lock (_itemIdentityToToken)`, calling `ConcurrentDictionary.TryAdd` plus performing `Array.Resize` while holding the lock causes substantial hold times, leading to thread pool convoying and high latency in `VBCSCompiler`.

---

### Additional related observation (AnalyzerExecutor `ConcurrentDictionary.Count`):
In the same trace, another contention hotspot during concurrent builds was found in `AnalyzerExecutor.OnOperationBlockActionsExecuted`:
```text
System.Collections.Concurrent.ConcurrentDictionary`2.AcquirePostFirstLock
  at System.Collections.Concurrent.ConcurrentDictionary`2.get_Count()
  at Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Diagnostics.AnalyzerExecutor.OnOperationBlockActionsExecuted(ImmutableArray`1<IOperation> operationBlocks)
```
In `AnalyzerExecutor.cs`:
```csharp
if (_lazyControlFlowGraphMap?.Count > 0) // <-- Calls ConcurrentDictionary.Count
```
`ConcurrentDictionary.Count` acquires **all** internal bucket/stripe locks (`AcquireAllLocks`). When multiple analyzer workers run concurrently in `VBCSCompiler`, this triggers severe lock convoying. Checking `_lazyControlFlowGraphMap?.IsEmpty == false` instead would eliminate this contention.
